WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:08.641
FAYE MOSS: Hello everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History , the talk show featuring famous people from

2
00:00:08.642 --> 00:00:11.137
the past. I'm your host,

3
00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:18.841
Faye Moss. Right now I'm dying for some chocolate, and it's all the fault of our guest

4
00:00:18.842 --> 00:00:28.279
today! He's the man who introduced Americans to chocolate, and he's famous for his philanthropy and the town that bears his name.

5
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:48.215
Straight from history, please welcome Milton Hershey!

6
00:00:48.216 --> 00:00:55.035
MILTON HERSHEY: Hello, Faye. May I offer you a Hershey's

7
00:00:55.036 --> 00:01:02.567
bar? FAYE MOSS: Thanks, Mr. Hershey! I can't eat during the show, so let's get this

8
00:01:02.568 --> 00:01:07.010
interview started! As a kid, did you dream of owning a

9
00:01:07.011 --> 00:01:17.594
chocolate factory? MILTON HERSHEY: Not at all. When I was born in rural Pennsylvania in 1857, most Americans hadn't

10
00:01:17.595 --> 00:01:25.493
even tasted chocolate. In the mid-nineteenth century, "candy" meant treats like taffy, caramels, and

11
00:01:25.494 --> 00:01:29.535
hard candies. I got in the business purely

12
00:01:29.536 --> 00:01:33.551
by accident. FAYE MOSS: That sounds intriguing!

13
00:01:33.552 --> 00:01:39.765
Tell us more. MILTON HERSHEY: My family moved around a lot because my father

14
00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:50.136
had trouble holding a job. I was never able to attend one school for very long, and by the time I was 14, my mother thought it would be better for

15
00:01:50.137 --> 00:01:56.506
me to work. So I quit school and became a printer's apprentice, but I hated

16
00:01:56.507 --> 00:02:02.004
that job. One day I decided to let my straw hat fall into a

17
00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:06.676
printing press. The press stopped working, and I got

18
00:02:06.677 --> 00:02:09.577
fired. FAYE MOSS: Did you find a job in

19
00:02:09.578 --> 00:02:19.487
a candy factory? MILTON HERSHEY: Not a factory. I spent the next four years at an ice cream and candy

20
00:02:19.488 --> 00:02:26.962
shop in Lancaster, Pennsylvania. I was good at making candy and learned so much that I opened my own

21
00:02:26.963 --> 00:02:37.322
shop in Philadelphia. It was 1876, and many people were visiting the Centennial Exposition—the first world's fair in the

22
00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:44.933
United States. I ran a pipe outside to tempt customers with the delicious smells of

23
00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:49.832
candy-making. Lots of them stopped in the shop on their way to the

24
00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:57.654
fair. As time passed, I expanded into the wholesale business and moved to a bigger shop.

25
00:02:57.655 --> 00:03:01.090
FAYE MOSS: It sounds like you were a

26
00:03:01.091 --> 00:03:05.366
success. MILTON HERSHEY: Yes,

27
00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:10.777
for half-a-dozen years. But then I made some bad decisions and had to

28
00:03:10.778 --> 00:03:15.455
close my business. The next year I opened another shop in New

29
00:03:15.456 --> 00:03:20.516
York City. When that one failed, I was forced to return to

30
00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:25.880
Lancaster. Maybe I'd have been more successful if I'd had more

31
00:03:25.881 --> 00:03:29.941
education. FAYE MOSS: I'm sure your family was delighted to

32
00:03:29.942 --> 00:03:33.859
see you. MILTON HERSHEY: Not delighted enough to help me

33
00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:45.622
start a new business! My mother's family had financed my Philadelphia and New York stores, but by now everyone thought I was as irresponsible

34
00:03:45.623 --> 00:03:50.533
as my father. I had to start my new venture—making caramels—on

35
00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:59.425
my own. When I received a check for a big order, I was so excited I ran to the bank wearing my

36
00:03:59.426 --> 00:04:04.520
apron. My factory eventually employed over 700

37
00:04:04.521 --> 00:04:07.954
people. FAYE MOSS: What motivated you to start

38
00:04:07.955 --> 00:04:13.346
making chocolate? MILTON HERSHEY: With the success of my caramel factory, I

39
00:04:13.347 --> 00:04:21.030
had money to travel. In Europe I tasted the finest Swiss milk

40
00:04:21.031 --> 00:04:28.189
chocolate—an expensive luxury. Unlike American chocolate, which was bitter and brittle, this was sweet

41
00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:36.600
and creamy. But the real turning point came in 1893, when I attended another world's fair

42
00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:46.091
in Chicago. A German company's exhibit demonstrated chocolate making—from roasting cacao beans to the finished product—right

43
00:04:46.092 --> 00:04:51.634
there. I kept returning to watch the machinery and sample the chocolate.

44
00:04:51.635 --> 00:04:55.802
I believed chocolate had a bigger future than caramels.

45
00:04:55.803 --> 00:05:05.224
When the fair ended, I purchased equipment from the exhibit and started a product line that included chocolate coatings and cocoa. FAYE

46
00:05:05.225 --> 00:05:08.416
MOSS: Did you also make a milk chocolate bar?

47
00:05:08.417 --> 00:05:16.753
MILTON HERSHEY: Not yet. No one in America had made milk chocolate before, so I had to figure it out

48
00:05:16.754 --> 00:05:22.347
myself. Fortunately I had always enjoyed experimenting with

49
00:05:22.348 --> 00:05:30.122
recipes. In 1900 I sold my caramel company for a million dollars to focus on chocolate.

50
00:05:30.123 --> 00:05:39.186
The first Hershey's milk chocolate was produced that year but could only be sold locally because it spoiled quickly. I

51
00:05:39.187 --> 00:05:44.216
hadn't figured out how to keep milk chocolate from spoiling yet. With

52
00:05:44.217 --> 00:05:52.033
a small group of employees, I worked night and day for several more years on this problem. We experimented

53
00:05:52.034 --> 00:05:56.491
with different ways of condensing milk. Finally we discovered

54
00:05:56.492 --> 00:06:07.651
a method that allowed us to mass-produce long-lasting chocolate bars at a price everyone could afford: five cents. FAYE MOSS: And they

55
00:06:07.652 --> 00:06:10.940
stayed a nickel for many years. Even before

56
00:06:10.941 --> 00:06:16.455
you had the perfect recipe, you were confident you'd succeed. You had already

57
00:06:16.456 --> 00:06:22.938
started building a giant chocolate factory—and a town for the workers! MILTON HERSHEY: That's right.

58
00:06:22.939 --> 00:06:24.374
I began

59
00:06:24.375 --> 00:06:33.237
construction on the world's largest chocolate factory in 1903, and it opened two years later. I wanted my

60
00:06:33.238 --> 00:06:41.746
employees to have a good place to live, so I oversaw every detail, including naming the streets. The main streets intersected

61
00:06:41.747 --> 00:06:51.168
at Chocolate and Cocoa Avenues, and other streets were named after cacao-growing areas. The houses were high quality

62
00:06:51.169 --> 00:06:59.702
and had indoor plumbing and electricity before many other American homes did. FAYE MOSS: Hershey, Pennsylvania even

63
00:06:59.703 --> 00:07:04.620
became a tourist attraction! MILTON HERSHEY: Yes, that's

64
00:07:04.621 --> 00:07:09.603
because Hershey was more than a factory town. There were

65
00:07:09.604 --> 00:07:13.276
parks, theaters, and even a zoo! FAYE MOSS: Tell

66
00:07:13.277 --> 00:07:15.895
us about the school you founded. MILTON

67
00:07:15.896 --> 00:07:23.774
HERSHEY: It was my dear wife's idea. Kitty was always sick, and we were unable to have children.

68
00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:29.156
In 1909 we founded a residential school for orphan boys. Unlike

69
00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:37.337
most orphanages where the children lived in dorms, our boys lived in cottages with house parents. In

70
00:07:37.338 --> 00:07:43.671
addition to their schoolwork, they were expected to learn a trade and milk cows. A chocolate

71
00:07:43.672 --> 00:07:46.628
factory needs lots of milk! FAYE MOSS: It

72
00:07:46.629 --> 00:07:50.448
sounds like the school was really important to you. MILTON

73
00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:54.910
HERSHEY: I wanted other boys to have the stability I never had.

74
00:07:54.911 --> 00:08:03.647
In 1918, three years after Kitty died, I donated my multi-million-dollar fortune to the school.

75
00:08:03.648 --> 00:08:10.380
Because I had no children, I decided the Hershey boys would become my heirs. FAYE

76
00:08:10.381 --> 00:08:16.598
MOSS: Thank you for sharing your story today—I'll think of you every time I eat chocolate!

77
00:08:16.599 --> 00:08:21.600
We'll let you get back to history now, and I'll enjoy the treat you brought me! MILTON

78
00:08:21.601 --> 00:08:26.652
HERSHEY: Thank you,

79
00:08:26.653 --> 00:08:35.252
Faye. FAYE MOSS: Milton Hershey continued to take an interest in the chocolate business and the school until he died

80
00:08:35.253 --> 00:08:40.300
in 1945. Originally only white male orphans were enrolled at the Milton Hershey

81
00:08:40.301 --> 00:08:46.807
School, but by the mid-twentieth century, the school was welcoming all low-income boys and

82
00:08:46.808 --> 00:08:57.982
girls. The town, which features street lights shaped like Hershey's Kisses and smells like chocolate, still draws tourists, especially to its amusement park.

83
00:08:57.983 --> 00:09:08.126
That's all for today. See you next time on Superstars in History !

